added_lemmas.statement:
Fix \(Q\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{att}}\), condition on the projected pilots, put \(x_\eta=\log(8/\eta)\) and \(M_h=mh^d\), and let \(T_*^\circ=\mathbb E_Q(T_*\mid\bar\pi,\bar\mu)\).  There are constants \(C_0,C_1<\infty\), depending only on \((d,\kappa,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L_\pi,L_\mu)\) and the fixed basis normalizations, such that, whenever \(1\le k\le M_h\) and \(M_h\ge C_0x_\eta\),
\[
 \Pr_Q\!\left[
 \lVert T_*-T_*^\circ\rVert_2>C_1\left\{
 \sqrt{\frac{x_\eta}{M_h}\left(1+\frac{k}{M_h}\right)}
 +\frac{x_\eta}{M_h}
 +\frac{\sqrt{k}\,x_\eta^{3/2}}{M_h^{3/2}}
 +\frac{kx_\eta^2}{M_h^2}
 \right\}\,\middle|\,\bar\pi,\bar\mu\right]
 \le\eta/2.
\]
The bound is uniform in the realized pilots and in \(J_\pi,J_\mu\).

added_lemmas.proof_tex:
Write \(r_p=p_\gamma^2+p_\gamma\), which is fixed, and consider one of the \(r_p\) scalar coordinates.  The corresponding linear kernel \(L_j\) satisfies, uniformly over the projected pilots,
\[
 |L_j|\le Ch^{-d},\qquad
 \mathbb E_Q L_j^2\le Ch^{-d}.                                \tag{6}
\]
The first inequality follows from bounded outcomes, overlap, and the bounded fixed-dimensional basis \(\rho_h\); the second additionally uses the uniform design and \(\operatorname{Leb}(C_h)=h^d\).  For a centered random variable \(Z\) with \(|Z|\le a\), expansion of the exponential series gives, for \(0\le t<3/a\),
\[
 \mathbb E e^{tZ}
 \le\exp\!\left\{\frac{t^2\mathbb EZ^2}{2(1-ta/3)}\right\}.  \tag{7}
\]
Applying (7) to the independent centered linear terms and optimizing in \(t\) yields, for every \(p\ge2\),
\[
 \left\lVert m^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^m
       \{L_j(O_i)-\mathbb EL_j(O_i)\}\right\rVert_{L^p}
 \le C\left(\sqrt{\frac p{M_h}}+\frac p{M_h}\right).          \tag{8}
\]

For the order-two term replace \(H_j(o,o')\) by its symmetrization and take its Hoeffding decomposition.  Its two first-order projections obey the same bounds (6): orthogonal projection is an \(L^2\)-contraction, while the boundary-corrected tensor spline projector is uniformly \(L^\infty\)-stable.  Thus their empirical averages also satisfy (8).

For completeness, we derive the moment bound for the canonical component.  Each coordinate of \(H\) is a bilinear kernel \(F(o)^{\mathsf T}G(o')\) through the \(k\)-dimensional projection kernel \(b_{h,k}(x)^{\mathsf T}b_{h,k}(x')\).  Orthonormality, uniform design, bounded multipliers, and
\[
 \iint_{C_h^2}\{b_{h,k}(x)^{\mathsf T}b_{h,k}(x')\}^2,dx,dx'=k
\]
give, for its canonical symmetrization \(H_{2,j}\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E H_{2,j}^2&\le Ckh^{-2d},\\
 \lVert H_{2,j}\rVert_{L^2\to L^2}&\le Ch^{-d},\\
 \sup_o\mathbb E\{H_{2,j}(o,O')^2+H_{2,j}(O',o)^2\}&\le Ckh^{-3d},\\
 \lVert H_{2,j}\rVert_\infty&\le Ckh^{-2d}.                  \tag{9}
 \end{aligned}
\]
Here the operator norm in the second line is that of the integral operator on \(L^2(P_Q)\).

To pass from (9) to a moment inequality, take an even integer \(2r\), expand the \(2r\)-th moment of \(\sum_{i\ne j}H_{2,j}(O_i,O_j)\), and associate to each monomial its multigraph on the sample indices.  Canonicality makes every graph with a degree-one vertex vanish.  Repeatedly remove, in this order, a disjoint doubled edge, a cycle, a vertex incident to at least three edges, and a fully repeated edge.  Cauchy--Schwarz and the four lines of (9), respectively, bound the resulting graph sums.  There are at most \((Cr)^r\), \((Cr)^{2r}\), \((Cr)^{3r}\), and \((Cr)^{4r}\) corresponding labelings.  Taking \(2r\)-th roots gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \left\lVert\sum_{i\ne j}H_{2,j}(O_i,O_j)\right\rVert_{L^p}
 \le C\{&m\sqrt{k p}\,h^{-d}+mp,h^{-d}\\
         &+\sqrt{mk h^{-3d}},p^{3/2}+kh^{-2d}p^2\},          \tag{10}
 \end{aligned}
\]
first for even \(p\), and then for all \(p\ge2\) by monotonicity of \(L^p\)-norms and replacement of \(p\) by the next even integer.  This finite moment expansion is the required order-two concentration calculation; no asymptotic or Gaussian approximation is used.

Since \(m\ge4\), division by \(m(m-1)\) in (10), together with \(M_h=mh^d\), yields
\[
 \left\lVert U_{2,j}\right\rVert_{L^p}
 \le C\left\{
 \frac{\sqrt{kp}}{M_h}+\frac p{M_h}
 +\frac{\sqrt{k},p^{3/2}}{M_h^{3/2}}
 +\frac{kp^2}{M_h^2}\right\}.                               \tag{11}
\]
Combining (8) and (11) proves the same bound for one coordinate of \(T_*-T_*^\circ\).  Because \(x_\eta>\log 32>2\), take \(p=c_{r_p}x_\eta\) in Markov's inequality
\[
 \Pr\{|Z|>e\lVert Z\rVert_{L^p}\}\le e^{-p}.
\]
The fixed constant \(c_{r_p}\) makes a union bound over all \(r_p\) coordinates at most \(\eta/2\), and equivalence of Euclidean, entrywise, and operator norms in the fixed output dimension only changes \(C_1\).  This proves the claim.  The only pilot properties used were their deterministic range and H\"older restrictions, so the constants are uniform in their realization and dimensions.

proposed_definition_changes.proposed:
Let \(x_\eta=\log(8/\eta)\) and \(M_h=mh^d\).  The corrected sampling envelope is
\[
 S(h,k,\eta)=C_S\left\{
 \sqrt{\frac{x_\eta}{M_h}\left(1+\frac{k}{M_h}\right)}
 +\frac{x_\eta}{M_h}
 +\frac{\sqrt{k}\,x_\eta^{3/2}}{M_h^{3/2}}
 +\frac{kx_\eta^2}{M_h^2}
 \right\}.
\]
Here \(C_S\) depends only on the displayed class radii, overlap, dimension, and fixed basis normalizations.
